WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.200
<v Instructor>So, now, let's talk</v>

2
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.760
about Kinesis Data Streams.

3
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.860
So, it's a service used to collect

4
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.840
and store streaming data in real-time.

5
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.640
So, the keyword you have to look out for

6
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.680
at the exam is really real-time.

7
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.080
So, here is a diagram to explain better.

8
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.400
So, we have real-time data.

9
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.600
What is real-time data?

10
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.300
Well, it's data that is created and used on the spot.

11
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.060
For example, whenever users click on a website,

12
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.320
it's called a click stream,

13
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.290
or when you have a device that's connected to the internet,

14
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.480
for example, a connected bicycle,

15
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.240
or when you have metrics and logs on a server

16
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.130
and you want to use them directly.

17
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.850
So, you want to send those into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams.

18
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.820
And to do so, we must use what's called producers.

19
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.650
So, producers can be either applications

20
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.330
and this is actually code you have to write

21
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.970
to take data from your website or from your devices

22
00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.730
and send it into Kinesis Data Streams,

23
00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.680
or for example, for metrics and logs,

24
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.513
you can install

25
00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:52.830
what's called the Kinesis Agent on your servers

26
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.900
and it will act as a producer

27
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.970
to Kinesis Data Streams as well.

28
00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.270
So, then, your data is going to be sent in real-time,

29
00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.240
so, as it happens into Kinesis Data Streams.

30
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.190
And the reason why we do so is that we want

31
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.340
to have consumer applications consuming this data

32
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.860
in real-time and leveraging it.

33
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.780
So, we have our application.

34
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:13.980
And again, you should write some code

35
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.200
to read from Kinesis Data Streams,

36
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.310
or you can have Lambda functions,

37
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.900
I can read from it as well.

38
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.910
You have something called Amazon Data Firehose,

39
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.250
that we'll see in a future lecture.

40
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.100
And as well as, for example, services

41
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.440
to do analytics on top of your streams,

42
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.740
such as the Managed Service for Apache Flink.

43
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.410
So, some features of Kinesis Data Streams.

44
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.300
So, your data can be retained

45
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.780
on the stream for up to 365 days.

46
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.940
And because the data is persisted,

47
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.940
you are able to reprocess to replay data by your consumers.

48
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.780
That means also that once you send data

49
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.310
into Kinesis Data Streams,

50
00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.930
you cannot delete it.

51
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.410
You have to wait for it to expire from a time perspective

52
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.523
for it to get deleted.

53
00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.240
Now, data up to 10 megabytes can be sent

54
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.260
into Kinesis Data Streams.

55
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.750
But the real case, the real typical use case,

56
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.230
is to have a lot of small real-time data.

57
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.270
Your data is going to be in order

58
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.570
if you send two data points with the same partition ID.

59
00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:13.710
So, it's a way for you to say

60
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.770
that these two data points are related in time

61
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.260
by sharing the same partition ID.

62
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.180
And you have security features,

63
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.070
such as at-rest KMS encryption

64
00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.890
and in-flight HTTPS encryption.

65
00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.390
So, if you want to write

66
00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.670
what I call an optimized producer application

67
00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.590
that is meant for high throughput,

68
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.800
you should be using the Kinesis Producer Library, KPL.

69
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.200
And to write an optimized consumer application,

70
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.470
you should use the Kinesis Client Library or KCL.

71
00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:44.010
Okay, now, your Kinesis Data Streams has two capacity mode.

72
00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.080
The first one is provision mode

73
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.840
in which you choose the number of shards on your stream.

74
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:49.980
So, what is a shard?

75
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.760
A shard is basically how big your stream is.

76
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.530
So, you can have one shard,

77
00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.780
but you can have a thousand shards if you wanted to.

78
00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.250
The more shard you have,

79
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.170
the more throughput you're going to have inbound.

80
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.450
So, that means you get one megabyte per second,

81
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.000
or a thousand records per second for each shard

82
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.650
in terms of capacity.

83
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.470
And each shard in terms of read capacity now,

84
00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:14.250
in terms of out-traffic is two megabytes per second.

85
00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:15.120
So, if you want

86
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.850
to send, for example, 10,000 records per second,

87
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.530
or 10 megabytes per second,

88
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.440
then you will need to scale to 10 shards.

89
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.370
Now, if you wanted to adjust the number of shards over time,

90
00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.630
you can scale manually

91
00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.150
to increase or decrease the number of shards.

92
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:30.990
And of course, you need

93
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.820
to be able to monitor your throughput

94
00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.910
to know how many shards you must have at any point of time.

95
00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:39.480
You're going to pay for each shard provisioned per hour.

96
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.910
The other mode is on-demand mode.

97
00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.350
So, here, you don't need to provision,

98
00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.590
or manage the capacity of your Kinesis Data Streams.

99
00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.420
You have a default capacity provision,

100
00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.910
which is about 4,000 records per second,

101
00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.070
or four megabytes in.

102
00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.210
And then, automatically,

103
00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.820
Kinesis Data Streams is going to scale based

104
00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.670
on the observed throughput during the past 30 days.

105
00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.970
So, instead, here, you're going to pay per stream per hour

106
00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.280
and you're going to be billed per the amount of data

107
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.250
that goes in and out of your Kinesis Data Streams.

108
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.170
All right, that's it, I hope you liked it.

109
00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.120
And I will see you in the next lecture.

